\description{
Algorithms for \code{\link[=solve]{solve()}} on SDE problems with weak (distributional)
order 2 or 1, as in StochasticDiffEq. They match the moments of the
solution rather than its path, and replace the Brownian increments by
discrete random variables.
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \code{DRI1()}, \code{RI1()}, \code{RI3()}, \code{RI5()}, \code{RI6()}: Debrabant and Roessler's
weak order 2 methods for Ito SDEs (adaptive for scalar, diagonal or one
dimensional noise); \code{RDI1WM()} (order 1), \code{RDI2WM()}, \code{RDI3WM()},
\code{RDI4WM()} (order 2) their methods with fewer stages.
\item \code{RS1()}, \code{RS2()}: Roessler's weak order 2 methods for Stratonovich SDEs.
\item \code{PL1WM()}, \code{PL1WMA()}: Platen's weak order 2 method (for general and for
additive noise).
\item \code{NON()}, \code{NON2()}, \code{COM()}: Komori's methods for Stratonovich SDEs with
non commutative and commutative noise.
\item \code{SIEA()}, \code{SIEB()}, \code{SMEA()}, \code{SMEB()}: Tocino and Vigo-Aguiar's weak
order 2 methods.
\item \code{W2Ito1()}: Tang and Xiao's efficient weak order 2 method.
\item \code{DRI1NM()}: DRI1 for non mixing diagonal and scalar noise.
\item \code{IRI1()}: RI1 with drift implicit (theta method) stages, for stiff drift.
}

With several noise channels the stage values use the k-th column of the
diffusion for the k-th Wiener process (the diagonal matrix of \code{g} for
diagonal noise). StochasticDiffEq differs here in three places: \code{RS1()}
and \code{RS2()} with general noise read the first column of the last two
diffusion stages, and \code{RS1()}, \code{RS2()} and \code{PL1WM()} with diagonal noise
add the k-th diffusion value to every component. For scalar noise and for
one Wiener process the results agree with StochasticDiffEq to rounding.

\code{constructDRI1()} and the other \verb{construct*()} functions return the
tableaus (\code{RoesslerRI}, \code{RoesslerRS}, \code{KomoriNON} and \code{KomoriNON2}
objects); \code{checkRIOrder()}, \code{checkRSOrder()} and \code{checkNONOrder()} test
the order conditions. \code{Ihat2()} is the approximation of the iterated
integral the methods use.
}
